linuxmuster-radius (7.3.4) lmn73; urgency=medium

  * The package is now built with debhelper (dpkg-buildpackage) instead of a
    hand-written script. It installs the same files with the same
    permissions as 7.3.3; the service, the CLI, the configuration and the
    upgrade path behave exactly as before. New are only the files
    /usr/share/doc/linuxmuster-radius/changelog.gz and copyright and the
    package's lintian overrides.
  * Architecture is now amd64 instead of all: the bundled Python environment
    contains compiled x86_64 libraries, so the package never worked on other
    architectures. apt upgrades an installed 7.3.x (all) to 7.3.4 (amd64)
    like any other version.
  * Depends on python3 (>= 3.12) and python3 (<< 3.13) instead of
    python3 (>= 3.12): the environment under /opt/linuxmuster-radius/venv is
    built for the Python 3.12 of Ubuntu 24.04. A plain "apt upgrade" then
    keeps the newer Python back (python3 stays at 3.12) instead of leaving a
    broken service; a full distribution upgrade ("apt full-upgrade",
    do-release-upgrade) removes this package instead, leaving its
    configuration and state in place, so it can be reinstalled once a
    matching build exists. The compiled libraries add libc6 and libgcc-s1.
  * Hardened dependency checks. The lockfile check and the build now read
    every line of the lockfiles the way pip does and refuse anything but
    pinned versions with their SHA-256 hashes; an indented or URL
    requirement and option lines such as --extra-index-url used to pass the
    check. Before anything from a lockfile is installed, in the build and in
    every CI job, every hash is verified against PyPI and the pins must be
    exactly the resolved closure of the declared dependencies: an extra
    package slipped into a lockfile, even with a genuine hash, is refused
    before its code can run. The resolver for that check, uv, is pinned by
    hash and verified first. The checks no longer depend on the environment
    of whoever runs them (PATH, an activated virtualenv). The build
    re-checks the finished environment against the lockfiles. Not caught: a
    pin moved to another genuine release, older or newer, that still
    satisfies the declared dependencies; only the review of the lockfile
    change shows it.
  * The post-installation script turns off git's background maintenance in
    the instance change log (/var/lib/linuxmuster-radius/instances) and no
    longer lets git look into /root/.config when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is
    inherited from the installing admin. A precaution taken over from
    linuxmuster-squid 7.3.4, whose configuration failed on such a
    maintenance process.
  * After publishing, the release job checks that GitHub marks the release
    immutable and fails otherwise, so the files of a release cannot be
    replaced unnoticed. A release also carries the source package (.dsc,
    source tarball with exactly the files tracked in git, .changes,
    .buildinfo).

 -- Kevin Stenzel <mail@kevin-stenzel.de>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 11:55:45 +0200

linuxmuster-radius (7.3.3) lmn73; urgency=medium

  * Reproducible builds with verified dependencies: the Python environment
    under /opt/linuxmuster-radius/venv is now installed only from lockfiles
    that pin every package, pip included, by version and SHA-256 hash, so
    every build of a release carries exactly the same, verified files
    instead of whatever PyPI served on the day of the build. The build
    container is pinned by digest, the CI actions by commit, and releases
    are published as drafts first so their assets can be made immutable.
  * The build only takes Python releases that have been public for at least
    a week. Two bundled libraries are therefore one release older than in
    7.3.2, whose versions were then only days old: idna 3.19 (was 3.20) and
    watchfiles 1.2.0 (was 1.3.0). Neither changes what the service does
    (watchfiles is only used by uvicorn's auto-reload, which is off; idna only
    for non-ASCII host names). All other library versions are those of 7.3.2;
    configuration, service and upgrade path are unchanged.

